Z||000004||Carrolles||08-22-2001||01:41 PM||||Most likely the alt. belt is slipping until it warms up.  It could be that there is condensation on the pulley from sitting overnight.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||08-22-2001||01:46 PM||||The squealing from my Duke was definitely the alternator.  After replacing the belt and water pump it continued to squeal.  It finally crapped out altogether and after I replaced it, no more squeal.  You could even hear a slight squeal when just turning it by hand.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Kasdan||08-22-2001||03:58 PM||||Sometimes it does, sometimes it dosent, sometimes it lasts seconds, sometimes minuites.  One time it stopped, then it started doing it every time i revved the engine...its not doing it at any specific time interval, its really random.  Ill replace the alt. when i get the car back, ill keeep you people posted<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, Holley Ram-Air<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||08-22-2001||04:58 PM||||Sounds just like mine did.  Sometimes you could go for days with no squeal, then it would squeal everytime you started the engine.  Sometimes you could rev the engine and then let off the gas quickly and it would stop, other times it would not.  Very sporadic.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||08-22-2001||09:53 PM||||Check for fluid leaks, in particular from the water pump weep hole (failed beaing), the thermostat housing to intake manifold gasket, and the harmonic balancer to timing chain cover seal (it will actually wear a groove into the balancer, and a replacement seal will probably not match the grooves in the balancer).  A sleeve from Fel-Pro is a quick, cheap fix for the balancer.  This was the cause of my squeal.  Oil and anti-freeze will glaze a belt in no time.<BR>Also, the alternator bracket on V-6's tends to break in the slot that has the adjuster bolt for the alternator belt, causing the belt tension to decrease gradually as the adjuster bolt slips.  <BR>If you have a rebuilt alternator, sometimes the adjuster bolt holes are stripped out, and you can never get it tight unless you put in a longer bolt with a nut/lockwasher.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fierowrecker||08-22-2001||11:05 PM||||Hey Tyler!<BR>Not sure on a GT, but my duke alternator lower bolt wore almost all the way through the alt case...<BR>Could never get it to stop squeeling, till I replaced the alternator...<BR>Gotta make sure the lower bolt is TIGHT!!!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Kasdan||08-23-2001||08:23 AM||||Basicly what i need to do to ensure that the squeal wont come back is a.)  replace the alt b.) replace the alt belt  c.) TIGHTEN all alt belts?<P><P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, Holley Ram-Air<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
